@meta
id: 21-21
title: Scenario — the audit trail
chapter: 21
type: scenario
skills: macro-across-files, macro-edit, macro-record, macro-count
par: 170
minutes: 15
vscode: search across files, open six results, paste a line above each, then a spreadsheet for the fixture
open: order.provider.ts
vimrc: set hidden | let @a="Oawait this.auditlog.record(id);\<Esc>"

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    PAY-212  Every repository update in the providers must be
             preceded by an audit record. Dana's macro is in the
             ticket. Convert the audit fixture while you are there.

  1. Every `this.<something>Repository.update({` call in the three
     providers gets this line directly above it, at the same indent:

         await this.auditLog.record(id);

     Six calls: two each in `order.provider.ts`, `refund.provider.ts`
     and `shipment.provider.ts`. Only the first is open.

     Dana's macro is already in register `a`; `:reg a` shows it. She
     typed `auditlog`. The property is `auditLog`. Fix the macro
     before you run it anywhere.

  2. `audit.fixture.ts` has eight rows pasted as `orderId,action`.
     Each becomes `{ orderId: 'ord_101', action: 'cancel' },`.

  3. `order.repository.ts` and `audit.service.ts` are in the
     directory. Neither changes. The repository defines `update`; it
     does not call it.

Finish with `:wqa`.

@keys
| `:reg a` | look at the macro first |
| `"ap`, fix, `0"ay$`, `dd` | paste it, correct it, yank it back |
| `:let @a="..."` | or just retype it |
| `:vimgrep /pattern/ *.provider.ts` | the six calls, and nothing in the repository |
| `:cdo normal @a` | the corrected macro at each of them |
| `:e file` | open the fixture |

@hints
Look at the macro before you touch anything. A one-letter fix to a
macro is a paste, a change and a yank, or a retyped `:let`. Either is
faster than recording it from nothing.
--
The grep pattern has to match the *calls*, not the method definition
in the repository. `Repository\.update(` does that: the definition is
`async update(` with no receiver in front of it.
--
`:cdo` puts the cursor on the hit; the macro's `O` opens a line above
it and autoindent handles the spacing. Nothing in the macro depends on
the column.
--
The fixture is lesson 3: `:e audit.fixture.ts`, record on the first
row, count the rest. Use a different register so `a` is still
available if you need it again.

@solution
One route.

**Fix the macro.** `Go<Esc>"ap` puts it on a scratch line. `0fl` lands
on the only lowercase `l` in it, `rL`. `0"ay$` then `dd`. Or, since it
is short, `:let @a="Oawait this.auditLog.record(id);\<Esc>"`.

Do not search for the typo. `/auditl` from the scratch line wraps to
the top of the file, and with `ignorecase` on it lands on `auditLog`
in the constructor. The fix goes on the wrong line and the macro stays
wrong. Chapter 12's warning about case-insensitive search, again.

**Run it everywhere.** `:vimgrep /Repository\.update(/ *.provider.ts`
then `:cdo normal @a`. Six hits, six new lines, three files, and you
never opened two of them.

**The fixture.** `:e audit.fixture.ts`, `j`, then
`qb^i{ orderId: '<Esc>f,s', action: '<Esc>A' },<Esc>jq` and `7@b`.

`:wqa`. About a hundred and fifty keystrokes.

Two things from the chapter meet here. The first is that a macro is
text, and someone else's macro is text you can read and correct
before trusting it. Read `:reg a` the way you would read a diff. The
second is that a macro plus a list is a refactor across the repo, and
the list can come from a grep. Nothing in the macro knew about files.

The habit worth keeping: a macro handed to you by a ticket, a
colleague or a blog post goes through `:reg` and a single test replay
before it goes through `:cdo`. `u` fixes one line. `:cdo` on a wrong
macro is six files of `u`.

@start order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { AuditService } from '../../infrastructure/services/audit.service';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected auditLog: AuditService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { AuditService } from '../../infrastructure/services/audit.service';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected auditLog: AuditService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.auditLog.record(id);
    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    await this.auditLog.record(id);
    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }
}

